<p>Apple can at last admit what everyone knew about its <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2025/02/apple-is-removing-its-advanced-data-protection-feature-in-the-uk/">restriction in February 2025</a> on iCloud&#8217;s <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2022/12/apple-to-extend-end-to-end-encryption-add-other-security-features/">Advanced Data Protection</a> (ADP) for new users in the United Kingdom: the government made us do it.</p>
<p>ADP provides end-to-end, device-based encryption for most iCloud data, and it was a remarkable bump up in privacy protection when Apple <a href="https://www.macworld.com/article/1439693/how-to-enable-manage-advanced-data-protection-icloud-data.html">released it suddenly in December 2022</a>. The February 2025 announcement offered no details as to why Apple made this enrollment change.</p>
<p>Reporting in April 2025 revealed that the UK government had demanded Apple build a backdoor for access to iCloud data under the Investigatory Powers Act 2016, which also prevented Apple from revealing this demand. Apple instead disabled the feature for new users.</p>
<p>On Thursday, <a href="https://investigatorypowerstribunal.org.uk/judgement/1-mr-vincent-kearney-2-british-broadcasting-corporation-v-1-chief-constable-of-the-police-service-of-northern-ireland-2-chief-constable-of-durham-constabulary-3-security-service-4-governme/">the UK Investigatory Powers Tribunal</a>, an independent body that allows challenges to government surveillance orders, said—paraphrasing—<a href="https://giftarticle.ft.com/giftarticle/actions/redeem/7d458e62-858a-4313-a16c-9ec3179ed12b">it was ridiculous for Apple to be unable to discuss something widely known in public</a>.</p>
<p>Apple challenged an original broad order that would have jeopardized privacy for users worldwide, and a second one that more narrowly set the scope for British citizens. The company told <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/19/u-k-government-forced-to-disclose-secret-apple-backdoor-order/">some media outlets</a> that it &#8220;welcomes the decision to make public the fact that the U.K. Home Office issued a technical capability notice.&#8221;</p>
